Z||000000||Ali_Gulf_Fiero||10-31-2001||01:28 PM||||I pulled a pair of aero rocker panels off a junked '86SE. Intact and ready to be refinished. One has a 1 square inch chunk taken out of it. My question is two-fold:<BR>1) Does this thermoplastic olefin stuff need primer or special paint to properly adhere?<BR>2) What kind of filler/resin should I use to bond cracks and the hole in the panel?<BR>I got these pieces for $20 per side. Fierostore's are $80-ish. So I feel I've gotten a bargain so far. Would like to refinish 'em and paint them. Any advice would be mucho helpful! Thanksabunch!<P>------------------<BR>my cat's sitting in my scrambled eggs.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Bazooka||10-31-2001||05:07 PM||||*******************************<BR>Ali,   You can add Flex Agent to the paint, But the modern paints like PPG are OK with proper surface preparation on the Thermo Plastic Olefin. Use the below filler with afiberglass cloth or screening for strength.<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.members.aol.com:/m34chev/images/smc.art">   <BR>*******************************<BR>   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ali_Gulf_Fiero:I pulled a pair of aero rocker panels off a junked '86SE. Intact and ready to be refinished. One has a 1 square inch chunk taken out of it. My question is two-fold:<BR>1) Does this thermoplastic olefin stuff need primer or special paint to properly adhere?<BR>2) What kind of filler/resin should I use to bond cracks and the hole in the panel?<BR>I got these pieces for $20 per side. Fierostore's are $80-ish. So I feel I've gotten a bargain so far. Would like to refinish 'em and paint them. Any advice would be mucho helpful! Thanksabunch!<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Bazooka (edited 10-31-2001).]||||reg||1
